00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.890
BRENNAN SAETA: Hi,
my name is Brennan.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.400
I'm an engineer on the
Brain Team at Google.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.070
And I work on cloud TPUs.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.220
So that's what we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.030
So to begin with, I wanted
to start with a little bit

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.020
of motivation.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:21.980
Why?

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:22.620
Why TPUs?

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.399
What are we here?

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:26.690
This is a chart that I'm
actually really quite fond of.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:31.190
It was recently published
by Open AI on their AI blog.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.050
And it traces over
the last six years

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.650
a number of seminal
advancements that have happened

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.580
in the field of deep learning.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.340
You can see on the
left AlexNet, which

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.930
is one of the ones
that the models

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.513
that really kicked off the deep
learning revolution that we're

00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:46.340
experiencing today.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.910
As you go farther
together the right,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.850
you can see more
advanced vision models.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.150
But you also see whole new
capabilities being unlocked.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.990
Translation, deep speech
with audio processing,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:02.030
neural machine translation,
Dota 1v1 real-time game playing,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.879
and one of my
favorites, AlphaGo Zero,

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.580
and Alpha Zero at the top right.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.629
And as deep learning
has progressed,

00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:11.420
we've gotten more and
more stuff that we're

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.190
able to do with deep learning,
which is really cool.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.670
But if you notice the
y-axis of the plot,

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.540
we need not just linearly
increasing amount of compute,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.550
we need exponentially
more compute.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.100
Unfortunately, just as we
need exponentially more

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.440
compute to do deep
learning and unlock

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.920
this world of possibilities,
Moore's Law, or in particular

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.160
we're running into the
end of Dennard scaling.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.120
Single core clock performance
is not improving very much.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.664
Single thread performance IPC
has basically hit a brick wall.

00:01:43.664 --> 00:01:45.080
Even though we're
still increasing

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.621
the number of
transistors, that trend

00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:48.690
is even starting to
slow down more recently.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.680
And so we're really in between
a rock and a hard place.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.430
And so we at Google, we
realized that deep learning

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.110
was so important.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.990
And we realized that it was
so broadly applicable that we

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.140
started investing in custom
accelerators all the way back

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:03.950
in--

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.720
even before 2015.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.639
So in the top left-hand corner
is our first generation TPU.

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:10.460
There are actually
these actual devices

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.640
available in the booth over
there if you're here in person.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.220
Our first generation
TPU was designed

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.680
just for inference
only, so serving a model

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:19.754
after it's been trained.

00:02:19.754 --> 00:02:21.170
This was very
important as we were

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.850
deploying our first, large-scale
deep learning models at Google.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.360
But over time, we realized that
training was just incredibly

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.834
hard to do and to do it well.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:34.000
And so we started investing
in our second generation

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.997
TPU, which you see
in the middle here.

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:37.580
But we realized that
in order to train

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.650
bigger, more powerful
models, we need

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.200
to have these TPUs
all linked together

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.940
and focus on a single task in
a very tightly coupled fashion.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.530
And so these TPU
devices are really

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.590
designed to be deployed in
TPU pods, which you can see

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.440
on our top right-hand corner.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.930
But deep learning is
advancing so rapidly,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.300
and there's so much
possibilities for this field.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.230
We've been iterating
extremely quickly.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.410
And at the bottom, our
third-generation TPU pod

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.550
is over eight times faster
and is available just less

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.890
than a year after our
second generation TPU pod.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.060
So we're really excited about
all that we can do with TPUs.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.090
And we're excited to bring them
to you as part of cloud TPUs.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.149
So that's our motivation
for why we're here.

00:03:19.149 --> 00:03:21.190
This is what I'm going to
be talking about today.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.450
The first is the life
of a TPU program.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.340
Let's dig into what
actually happens

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.030
underneath the hood when
you're running on a cloud TPU.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.630
The second part is what do you
want to do, how do you actually

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.535
program these TPU devices.

00:03:34.535 --> 00:03:37.160
And the third part, we'll spend
a few minutes talking about one

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.745
of the most common
stumbling blocks

00:03:38.745 --> 00:03:40.370
that people run into
when they're first

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.990
using TPUs, which is how to
keep them fed fast enough.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.310
They're just so fast.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.940
It really is an interesting
engineering challenge

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.020
to feed data.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.170
So with no further ado,
let's dig right in.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.710
So if you want to run a
program on a cloud TPU,

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:58.320
well, you've got to provision
your cloud TPU resources.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.500
And so let's talk a
little bit about that.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.480
We show this picture of like
one cloud TPU device, right?

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.220
This is our
second-generation TPU device.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.070
But really you never
see them bare like this

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:11.340
in our data center.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.845
These are specialized devices.

00:04:12.845 --> 00:04:14.470
And they're not
general purpose, right?

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.303
They're optimized just
for machine learning.

00:04:16.303 --> 00:04:19.130
So in practice, we
deploy them often

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.440
in what we call these
pod configurations.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.080
So here you've got two
middle racks of TPUs.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.620
You actually have an 8-by-8
or 64 total cloud TPU

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.340
devices hooked together in
this custom mesh network.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.680
And flanking these 64
TPU devices are actually

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.480
64 general purpose x86
machines in racks a and d.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.500
And their job is to feed
and manage the TPU core.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.180
When you request
one cloud TPU, you

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.770
don't get just the
TPU device itself.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.880
You also get the dedicated
server for that TPU device.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.480
So one cloud TPU is actually
two pieces of hardware,

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.367
often in two separate racks.

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:57.200
OK, so that's what the
physical hardware is.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.740
How does this look like from
a cloud platform perspective?

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.230
I'm going to talk about
TPUs on Compute Engine,

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.688
but this applies for the other
higher-level products, which

00:05:05.688 --> 00:05:07.890
we'll talk about
later in the talk.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.570
But if you have a bunch of
Compute Engine VMs running

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.650
in your private network,
if you're like me

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.700
you'll often store your data
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:17.910
in a storage bucket.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.120
This means it's available
to multiple GCVMs.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.350
It's really cheap,
really scalable.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.060
And when you want to allocate
a cloud TPU, what happens

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.860
is you get the dedicated
cloud TPU host and TPU device.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.730
And in order to access
them, we open up

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.407
this network peering
channel, or this bridge.

00:05:34.407 --> 00:05:35.990
And once that's open,
you can actually

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:39.560
connect to it from your GCEVMs
over TensorFlow's open source

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.837
GRPC protocol.

00:05:41.837 --> 00:05:44.420
Now in this picture, I'm showing
one Compute Engine VM talking

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.924
to one cloud TPU, but this is
designed for maximum control

00:05:47.924 --> 00:05:48.590
and flexibility.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.910
So you can have multiple VMs
talking to one cloud TPU.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.680
You can have one VM controlling
multiple cloud TPUs,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.520
or any combination you want.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.460
Cool.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:05:59.970
So how do you make that happen?

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.200
Well, it's just a couple
of G Cloud commands away.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.300
gcloud compute instances
create to create your GCVM.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.170
gcloud compute tpus
create to create your TPU.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:09.880
SSH in your VM,
install TensorFlow,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.910
and you're off to the races.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:13.160
Well, not quite so fast.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.784
These are sort of
complicated commands.

00:06:14.784 --> 00:06:16.659
Let's break it
down a little bit.

00:06:16.659 --> 00:06:18.200
When you create a
cloud TPU, you need

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.780
to specify the version
of TensorFlow installed.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.480
And the reason for that is
because TensorFlow is not

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.737
designed to work across
different versions.

00:06:25.737 --> 00:06:28.070
If you're running a distributed
TensorFlow cluster where

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.770
one node is running
TensorFlow 1.8 and one node

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.870
is running TensorFlow
1.9, you're

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.410
going to have a really bad day.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.370
The errors are absolutely
unintelligible.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.950
So when you create
a cloud TPU, you

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:42.120
need to specify what version
of TensorFlow you want to use.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.700
Additionally, you
have to specify

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:47.300
what network address range you
want to give over to the TPU

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.910
so that the TPU can be
peered onto your network.

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:51.880
And so you can see
the list of routes.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.980
Pick a route that is
unoccupied, and go for that.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.680
Now this is sort of involved
and requires too much thinking.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.940
And so because I create
a lot of cloud TPUs

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.120
as part of using them, I
created a tool called ctpu.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.420
Now ctpu is short for the
cloud TPU provisioning utility.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.390
And if you just run ctpu
up, it will basically

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:12.473
set everything up for you.

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:15.170
Launch a GCVM with
TensorFlow pre-installed,

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.930
create your cloud TPU, make sure
it's peered up appropriately,

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:21.800
and it'll even SSH you into your
GCVM, get you all ready to go.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.290
One thing to note. ctpu up
is designed to be idempotent.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.880
So if you have like a
GCVM already running

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.819
and your preemptible
cloud TPU got preempted,

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:30.860
you want to get things
back into a working state,

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:31.940
just rerun ctpu up.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.250
It'll SSH you back in.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:34.880
Cool.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.920
So now that we have the
cloud resources, let's

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.060
actually run something
on our cloud TPU, right?

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.420
The whole point of this.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.220
I would be remiss if
I did not emphasize

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.140
the availability of
our reference models

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.760
for cloud TPUs.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.140
They span a wide variety
of vertical domains.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:50.690
They are tested.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:51.987
They work really well.

00:07:51.987 --> 00:07:53.320
Some of them are even commented.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:54.985
It's really nice.

00:07:54.985 --> 00:07:56.360
They are available
on open source

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.760
on GitHub and on
our cloud TPU VMs.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.600
If you provision a
VM with ctpu, they're

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:04.460
available on the file
system at user share TPU.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.650
But this is the TensorFlow
cloud TPU deep dive.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.400
So we're going to ignore
these big fancy scripts,

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.184
and we're going to go down
to the basest, barest level.

00:08:13.184 --> 00:08:14.600
And so we're going
to ignore those

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.390
for the rest of this talk.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.450
TensorFlow underneath its
hood is a distributed graph

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.730
execution engine.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.940
You build up a computational
graph, often with Python.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.150
You pass it to the core
TensorFlow distributed runtime.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:30.870
And you then execute that graph.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:35.539
So here we have a graph for
a two-layer neural network.

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:37.470
And you can see the
graph executing.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.679
And so what we're
going to do is we're

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:45.105
going to build a simple graph
and execute it on a TPU.

00:08:45.105 --> 00:08:46.480
So this is a
technical deep dive,

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.230
so we're going to see
a lot of code today.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.110
Here is a very
simple implementation

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.690
of a linear aggressor
using TensorFlow.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.790
So we first describe
our stateful nodes

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.250
in our graph, our
variables w and b.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.870
These are the values
we want to learn.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.990
We then take as input x and y.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:07.290
These are our training data
and our training labels.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.710
We compute our predicted values,
y p with a matrix multiply,

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.560
because x and y are
batches of training values.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.866
We compute the loss by
doing the squared error.

00:09:16.866 --> 00:09:18.240
And we want to
minimize the loss,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.073
so we want to take
advantage of TensorFlow's

00:09:20.073 --> 00:09:22.980
automatic differentiation and
gradient update capabilities

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.315
with our optimizer.

00:09:24.315 --> 00:09:26.610
We're using Atom, which is
a variation on a momentum

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.790
optimizer, highly recommended.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.210
So this is a linear aggressor
in low-level TensorFlow.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.830
How do you make
it work on a TPU?

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.788
Well as it turns out,
you don't change anything

00:09:36.788 --> 00:09:38.070
about your model.

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.390
You keep that all the same.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:40.848
But what you need
to do is you need

00:09:40.848 --> 00:09:44.840
to tell TensorFlow hey, this
part, make this go on the TPU.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.630
And so to do that you pass your
model function, the part that

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:51.320
describes your model that
you want to run on the TPU,

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.640
you pass that to this
tf contrib TPU rewrite.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.070
And what this will do is
this will tell TensorFlow

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.830
put this part of the
computational graph

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.045
that I'm building onto the TPU.

00:10:02.045 --> 00:10:03.420
Once you've done
that, you've now

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.310
built a TensorFlow graph that
is set up to run on a TPU.

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:08.320
So now let's actually run it.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.830
To run a computational
graph in TensorFlow,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.000
you've got to create
a TensorFlow session.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.110
But you can't pass the TPU
name to the TensorFlow session

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:17.610
constructor.

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:18.750
It doesn't know how to--

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.000
standard, open-source
TensorFlow doesn't know anything

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.980
about cloud TPUs.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:24.667
So how do you make this work?

00:10:24.667 --> 00:10:26.250
Well, we have this
thing in TensorFlow

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.720
called cluster resolvers.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.250
So we have a TPU
cluster resolver

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.740
where you pass that, the
name of your cloud TPU.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:34.930
It will, on your behalf,
contact the cloud TPU API,

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.740
figure out all the metadata
about your cloud TPU,

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.120
and take care of all the
underlying details for you.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.250
So to create a TensorFlow
session on a cloud TPU,

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.060
you just create the
cluster resolver

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.690
and you pass that to
your session constructor.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.310
One thing to note.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:54.780
If you are using ctpu
or you're running

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.360
on Kubernetes Engine
or other environments,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.250
the TPU closer resolver
is clever enough

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.590
to inspect your
environment to figure out

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.740
what your TPU name probably is.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:06.210
And so you can pass none, and
it will try and figure out

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:07.570
what actually should be there.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:08.670
So this is really
convenient if you're

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.295
sharing scripts across
multiple people,

00:11:10.295 --> 00:11:12.880
or sharing multiple cloud TPUs.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:13.380
Cool.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.880
So now that we have the session,
it's really straightforward

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.090
to actually run our
program on the cloud TPU.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.570
So here we're doing
random variables,

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:22.950
because this is a toy example.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:26.220
We initialize the TPU
system, and then we're

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.270
sort of off to the races.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:28.186
Now one thing to note.

00:11:28.186 --> 00:11:31.090
This TPU initialized system
is relatively heavyweight.

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:34.110
So it's going to clear the
entire TPU system state,

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.030
interrupts any
running TPU programs

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.680
that are running
on the TPU devices,

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.160
rebuilds the mesh network, and
gets everything ready and clean

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:42.900
for you to use.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.480
After that, here we're going to
run 100 steps of our training

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.930
loop, print out the losses,
and shut down the system.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.560
So if you were to paste all
this into fu.py and type Python

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.290
fu.py enter, you'll
see your cursor blink

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.190
for like 10 or 15 seconds.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.000
And then 100 lines of
loss will be printed out.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.440
So those 10 or 15
seconds, that's

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.790
initializing the TPU system.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.191
We're working on making
that go a little bit faster.

00:12:05.191 --> 00:12:06.690
But then after that,
the computation

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.670
runs really quickly.

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.670
OK, so we just talked
about how we programmed it

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:13.860
from a low-level standpoint,
but what actually

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.730
happened inside the cloud TPU?

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:22.030
Cloud TPUs, the TPU chips,
they're not x86 chips.

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.430
You can't run a standard
program that you get out

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:26.440
of GCC or Clang.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.780
And so in order to run a
program on a cloud TPU,

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.520
you need to have a compiler.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.230
And we use the XLA compiler.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:32.980
So what is XLA?

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.710
XLA stands for Accelerated
Linear Algebra.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.290
And it's this custom,
domain-specific compiler that

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:42.130
takes in high-level,
pure functional programs

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:46.660
in a language called HLO And
it outputs optimized binary

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:51.480
programs for either
CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.350
Now XLA, it has a
few restrictions.

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:55.690
It's a linear, high-level
linear algebra-based language.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:59.050
You've got matrix multiplies,
convolutions, broadcast,

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.600
reduce some ops,
et cetera, but you

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.510
don't have very branchy
things like jpeg

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:07.990
de-code or other sorts
of low-level operations.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:11.076
Additionally, you have to--
we require static shapes.

00:13:11.076 --> 00:13:12.700
But in exchange for
these restrictions,

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.280
we give you a few super powers.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.710
The first one is
operation fusion.

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:20.840
Now operation fusion I think is
best illustrated in an example.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.410
If you're trying to write a
program to some three very

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:26.470
large matrices, but
your matrix library only

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:29.230
has support for summing
two matrices together,

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:31.810
well, you can work around
that by doing a plus b

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:33.580
and write that to
a temporary value.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:37.059
And then do temporary plus c
and get your eventual value.

00:13:37.059 --> 00:13:39.100
And you can do that, but
it's a performance cost.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.920
You've got to read and
write this temporary value.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:45.010
And it ends up being a very
significant performance cost.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:47.530
XLA, because it sees
your whole program

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:50.230
and it optimizes
everything, it can actually

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:54.691
give you the optimal program
without having you specify it.

00:13:54.691 --> 00:13:55.940
So that's one XLA super power.

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:57.481
And what this gives
you is this means

00:13:57.481 --> 00:13:59.410
you can write your own
composite operations.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:03.350
You aren't stuck into restricted
kernels that only work well

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:04.960
for certain hardware devices.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.040
You can invent
your own primitives

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:10.630
that then get compiled to
high-performance programs that

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:13.120
run on the TPU.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.300
Additionally, because XLA is
a whole program optimizer,

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.367
and it's in this pure
functional language,

00:14:18.367 --> 00:14:20.200
this means we can
actually not just optimize

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:23.260
the instructions that get
executed on your processor,

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.840
but this means we can optimize
the memory layouts as well.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.330
So I think this
most often comes up

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.590
if you're doing convolutional
networks on GPUs today.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.030
The recommendation was to do
what's called channels first

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:36.790
for most GPUs.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:39.910
You got much better performance
if you did NCHW right?

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.364
Batch channels height, width.

00:14:42.364 --> 00:14:44.530
But actually on the latest
Volta's with their tensor

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.600
cores, you get far
better performance

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.270
if you go back to the standard
TensorFlow image format, which

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.750
is channels last NHWC.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.970
With XLA, you never
have to worry about

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:56.890
what is your memory shape
formats or whatever.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:59.680
It will pick the optimal
format for your hardware

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.750
based on what program
you're trying to do,

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:04.119
compile your program
just for you.

00:15:04.119 --> 00:15:06.160
The final thing that XLA
does is it does software

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.060
pipelining really, really well.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:10.970
And we'll see why that's
important a little bit later.

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.147
So fine, XLA is great, cool.

00:15:13.147 --> 00:15:14.230
What does it matter to me?

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.280
I'm a TensorFlow
programmer, right?

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:16.930
I don't want to
write XLA programs.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:19.470
Well, that's why we have
what's called the TensorFlow

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:20.890
to XLA bridge.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.650
And what this will do is this
will take a TensorFlow graph

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:27.250
and this will convert
some part of your graph

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:28.630
to be an XLA program.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:31.190
So how this works is we've
got these TF to XLA kernels.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:35.740
So you've marked out in the
rewrite for TPU function.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:38.290
You've marked out a subset
of your TensorFlow graph

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:40.130
to run on the TPU.

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:42.100
So what we'll do is
we'll go through op by op

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.380
and translate them into
portions of the XLA program.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:47.390
Here are the XLA graph
on the right-hand side.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.990
For add, it's a straightforward
one-to-one mapping.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:53.500
But for softmax, there is no
softmax, what's called HLO, op.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:57.340
And so instead the kernel
builds up the softmax operation

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.602
from the set of primitives.

00:15:59.602 --> 00:16:01.060
What this means is
that if you want

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.060
to write your own operation
that's different than softmax,

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:05.980
you can build it up
from your own primitives

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.544
and it will run just
as fast as softmax

00:16:08.544 --> 00:16:10.210
modular, the performance
characteristics

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:11.840
of your computation, right?

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.486
There's nothing special.

00:16:13.486 --> 00:16:15.860
Once we finish translating
the whole program into the XLA

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:19.210
graph, the TensorFlow runtime
will execute the XLA compiler

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:20.710
on the XLA program
that it built up

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:23.380
for you, replace that
subset of your graph

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.330
with this custom XLA
program, and then it

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.590
will run the graph
just like you'd expect.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:33.470
OK, so that tells us what
bits of software are involved.

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.240
But like, let's dig
one level deeper.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.450
What actually happened
on the TPU chips?

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:39.950
In order to understand
that, we need

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.960
to understand what
are the TPU chips.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.970
We need to see what's
going on with them.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:46.670
So one TPU chip,
this is a V2 chip.

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:50.180
The V3 chip has double
the amount of HBM

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.290
and is little bit
faster in the core.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:56.300
But in a V2 chip,
you've got two cores.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:57.630
Each core is identical.

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.390
So let's double click
on one of the cores

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.620
and see what's going on.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:03.360
You've got three key components.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.000
You've got the
scalar unit, you've

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.310
got the vector unit,
and the matrix unit.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.770
So the scalar unit,
that's typically

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.714
used for loop
indexing, making sure

00:17:11.714 --> 00:17:13.130
if you're doing a
loop n times you

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:16.130
do it n times, computing
addresses and memory, things

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.410
like that.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:21.230
The vector unit is
very sophisticated.

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:23.430
It has support for float 32.

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:28.460
This is IEEE float32, int32,
this funky format, bfloat16,

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:29.949
which we'll hear
about in a minute.

00:17:29.949 --> 00:17:31.490
Through software it
can support a lot

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:35.210
of other formats like int64,
complex 64, et cetera,

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:38.070
where you've got an int32
real part, an int32 imaginary,

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:39.660
et cetera.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:41.900
And it lets you do
all sorts of things,

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:45.210
compute transcendentals,
things like that.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:49.520
But the real heart of the
cloud TPU is the matrix unit.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:52.580
So this is a special
hardware circuit

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:55.310
design called a systolic array.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:58.760
And so the matrix unit is a
128 by 128 matrix multiply

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:00.430
systolic array.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:03.900
And it is optimized
for machine learning.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:08.540
It takes in as input bfloat16,
or float32 values, computes

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:11.360
the multiplication in
this format bfloat16,

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:15.350
sums up the values in
float32, and the output

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:17.509
is then in float32.

00:18:17.509 --> 00:18:19.550
So for those of you who
need a bit of a refresher

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:21.530
course on IEEE
floating point formats,

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:25.700
I know I certainly did, here's
my brief handy dandy guide.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:28.100
So IEEE defined what's
called single precision

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:29.210
floating point format.

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:33.350
And this uses 32 bits and
is often called FP 32.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:35.000
You've got one sign bit.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:36.920
You've got 8 bits of exponents.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.330
And then the rest
of the 32 bits,

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:39.877
so you've got 23
bits left, that's

00:18:39.877 --> 00:18:41.210
the mantissa or the significant.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:43.190
And that's sort of like
how precise of a number

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:45.500
you're able to specify.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:49.370
Now IEEE also defined
a 16-bit half precision

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:50.480
floating point format.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:54.410
And this is sort of the very
natural extension from FP 32.

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:56.610
You take a few bits
away from the exponent,

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.410
you take a few bits
away from the mantissa.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:02.600
Bada bing bada boom, you've got
16-bit floating point format.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.900
One thing to note, however,
is that the range ends up

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:08.510
being quite drastically reduced.

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.700
And when you train
neural networks,

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:12.692
you'll find that if you
want to use float16,

00:19:12.692 --> 00:19:14.150
and there are a
lot of good reasons

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:17.775
why you want to use a 16-bit
floating point format,

00:19:17.775 --> 00:19:19.400
you have to do a
bunch of clever tricks

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.660
in order to make sure
it keeps training.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:24.800
If you-- most times when you
train a neural network in FP 16

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.390
without any modifications, your
losses will explode to infinity

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:31.262
or you'll get not a
number, NaNs, or your model

00:19:31.262 --> 00:19:31.970
just won't train.

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:35.450
You'll have vanishing
gradients that go down to zero.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.420
And this is because
neural networks really

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.915
need a large range in order
to often train effectively.

00:19:40.915 --> 00:19:43.749
And we recognize this
at Google, because we

00:19:43.749 --> 00:19:45.040
train a lot of neural networks.

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:47.572
And so we did a bunch of
studies across a broad range

00:19:47.572 --> 00:19:49.030
of neural networks,
and we realized

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:53.030
that if you have a different
floating point format,

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.420
this can often be a
drop-in replacement.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.100
So we invented, on
the Google Brain

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.710
team, the bfloat16 format.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:02.660
This is short for brain floating
point format in 16 bits.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:07.330
And this preserves the
exact same range of FP 32,

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:09.250
but does it in 16 bits.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:12.400
And as it turns out, this is
often a drop-in replacement

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:13.660
for FP 32.

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:16.570
But because it's only in 16
bits, it takes half the space.

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:18.970
That means your memory
feels twice as large.

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:22.604
This means that your memory
bandwidth is twice as fast.

00:20:22.604 --> 00:20:24.520
So it's a really cool
performance optimization

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:28.010
that we've done in TPUs.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:28.510
Cool.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:29.870
So now that we know about
floating point formats,

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.078
let's talk a bit more about
the matrix multiply unit.

00:20:32.078 --> 00:20:34.850
This is a 128 by 128
matrix multiply unit.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:36.340
So does that mean
that my model has

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:39.860
to be composed entirely of
128 by 128 matrix multiplies?

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:41.230
Well, the answer is no.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:44.020
So here's a study on RestNet-50.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:47.120
And I took a single cloud TPU
device that you can run today

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:51.850
and I basically sweeped a
number of different batch sizes.

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.580
And this is per core batch size.

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:56.090
So remember, a TPU
device has eight cores.

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:58.440
So this is our per
core batch size.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:01.000
As you go from one
to eight, you'll

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.670
see performance is increasing
very significantly.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:07.310
We reach our peak performance
at batch size 128.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:08.350
So what's going on here?

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.120
The XLA compiler
is able to realize

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:12.040
that if your batch
size is 128, that

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:15.562
fits really nicely with the 128
by 128 matrix multiply unit.

00:21:15.562 --> 00:21:17.650
But to really understand
what's going on,

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:20.980
I think it's easiest if we
plot it on a log scale, right?

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:22.600
We're doubling the
per core batch size

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:24.800
on the y ax-- on the x-axis.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:28.136
So let's turn the y-axis also
into a logarithmic scale.

00:21:28.136 --> 00:21:29.760
And here it's little
bit easier to see.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.930
We've got two distinct trends.

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:35.010
It's a piecewise
performance layer.

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:38.325
We increase performance
practically linearly from batch

00:21:38.325 --> 00:21:40.650
size one to batch size 8.

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:44.160
But after that, performance
improves only a little bit.

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.460
What this means is that XLA is
able to do a really good job

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.400
software pipelining all the
optimizations after you get

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.720
to batch size 8 on a cloud TPU.

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:54.362
Now this is just on RestNet-50.

00:21:54.362 --> 00:21:56.070
There have been a
variety of other models

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.510
that work really, really well
at even smaller batch sizes,

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.240
depending upon
whatever things are.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.730
So one question we
often get is do I

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:04.420
need to have my model
be batch size 120,

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:06.930
or do you need to have my
model be like 128 everywhere?

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:08.530
The answer is, of course, no.

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:10.730
XLA takes care of getting
you the optimal code

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:13.740
generation across a variety of
different model architectures

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:16.007
and batch sizes.

00:22:16.007 --> 00:22:18.340
OK, so now that we know a
little bit about the chip, now

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:19.690
we can really delve
down and figure out

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:22.060
what actually happened in
our linear aggressive program

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.860
on a TPU.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:25.780
So the first step, if
you load the weights

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:29.020
into our matrix multiply
unit, after that we stream

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:31.480
through our training
data into the matrix

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:33.760
multiply unit, compute
the matrix multiply,

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.830
and stream the output out.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:37.630
Now I mentioned about
Operation Fusion.

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:41.620
XLA is actually able to, in
parallel, stream the values

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:45.010
into the vector unit, compute
the loss or the activations

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:48.850
or whatever other operations
your model requests,

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:51.780
and stream those
values out in parallel.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.280
And as a result, you get
really high performance,

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:55.600
and you're really able
to take advantage of all

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:56.830
the capabilities on the chip.

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:02.834
So we've been delving really
deep into one single program,

00:23:02.834 --> 00:23:04.250
but as it turns
out all we've been

00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:08.190
doing is running on a single
core of our cloud TPU device.

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:10.157
TPUs are designed to
be networked together.

00:23:10.157 --> 00:23:11.990
And they're designed
to be parallel devices.

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:15.627
So multi-core execution
is very important.

00:23:15.627 --> 00:23:17.960
As it turns out, because we
have this custom networking,

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:20.084
because we've designed
these things from the ground

00:23:20.084 --> 00:23:23.570
up for parallel compute,
it's actually really easy

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:27.230
to make these go, not just on
a single core, but across cores

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:29.180
up to a whole pod.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.430
All you need to do is you
need to take your optimizer

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:34.640
and wrap it in this TPU
cross shard optimizer.

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:37.280
What this means is this
coordinates the optimization

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:40.520
routines that are
being run on your model

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.280
across all the different
compute cores in a TPU device

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.770
all the way up to
a whole TPU pod.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:49.010
After that, you just say batch
parallel instead of rewrite.

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:52.000
So this is a higher-level API.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.940
Specify the number
of shards, and done.

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:55.000
You're off to the races.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.125
That is all you need to do.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:02.817
So we've been walking through
how to write a TPU program.

00:24:02.817 --> 00:24:04.400
We've been using
low-level TensorFlow.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.024
This is because we
wanted to see what's

00:24:06.024 --> 00:24:08.030
going on underneath the hood.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:13.040
But really no one writes
low-level TensorFlow on TPUs.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:14.460
It is too verbose.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.060
The low-level APIs we're
optimizing and changing

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:18.770
relatively frequently,
it means you're

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:21.969
locked into a TPU program.

00:24:21.969 --> 00:24:24.260
There's a lot of features
and performance optimizations

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:26.870
that are also very hard to
write in low-level TensorFlow.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:30.080
So how do we program TPUs?

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:34.940
The first thing I want to tell
you about is TPU estimator.

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:37.610
So TensorFlow is designed
to go across a wide variety

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:40.119
of platforms that go from
research to production.

00:24:40.119 --> 00:24:41.660
And many of our
production users have

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:43.940
realized that many
of their problems

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:48.770
can be decomposed
into two key aspects.

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:50.660
The first aspect is
describing your machine

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:52.630
learning model,
this random forest,

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:55.250
a convolutional neural
network, a deep neural network.

00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:58.320
And these models are often
generic across the input data.

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:00.260
So you write a
second input function

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:02.670
that describes what data
you want to load in.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:07.040
And by decomposing this into
these two distinct parts,

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:10.130
you then pass these functions
to their core estimator logic,

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:12.860
and the estimator takes care
of all the nitty gritty things

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:14.870
like handling failures,
checkpointing,

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:17.240
exporting for inference, a
whole bunch of other really

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:18.600
nice things.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.120
And so the TensorFlow
estimator API

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:25.144
is used by a vast majority of
production teams at Google.

00:25:25.144 --> 00:25:27.560
And so we wanted to make it
really easy to take your model

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:30.890
and go from estimators
to TPU estimators

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:33.830
whether you're running on
CPUs and GPUs before or TPUs.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:37.190
So here's a code sample of
actually a full-working,

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.470
estimator-based model.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:42.590
So I've sort of
masked out the bottom.

00:25:42.590 --> 00:25:45.770
On the top, this
is identical code

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:49.555
if you're running with standard
estimators or TPU estimators.

00:25:49.555 --> 00:25:50.930
At the top of the
model function,

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:53.570
we describe our
deep neural network.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:54.944
We have our optimizer.

00:25:54.944 --> 00:25:57.110
On the right-hand side we've
got our input function.

00:25:57.110 --> 00:25:59.530
So we're loading data
from a path on disk.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:02.840
We're parsing out
our TF record values

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:06.380
and returning a data set
that has the training

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:08.750
data we want to train on.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:10.790
The only changes are
in the bottom half.

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:12.860
And aside from a few
mechanical changes,

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:15.590
changing standard
estimator to TPU estimator

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:19.190
and standard estimator
spec to TPU estimator spec,

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.398
there's only two small changes.

00:26:21.398 --> 00:26:22.820
[COUGHS]

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:24.330
Excuse me.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.170
The first one is, as
we saw before, you'll

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:30.290
want to run across multiple
cores on a TPU device.

00:26:30.290 --> 00:26:33.980
Your TPU device is way faster
when they all work together.

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:36.530
So you've got to use that
cross shard optimizer.

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:39.290
The only other thing is to
pass the TPU cluster resolver

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:41.870
that we saw before so the
estimator knows how to find

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:44.270
your TPU on the network.

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:45.170
That's it.

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:47.780
It's really easy to go
from an estimator that

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:51.290
runs on CPUs or GPUs to an
estimator-- to a TPU estimator

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:55.610
that will run on TPUs all the
way up to a whole TPU pod.

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:57.930
But I want to actually
call out one thing.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.860
We on the TensorFlow
team, we want

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:02.320
to go wherever you want to
go for your machine learning

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:03.350
workloads.

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:07.810
This means CPUs, this means
GPUs, and this means TPUs.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.630
And so we've worked very hard
so that if you just pass,

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:14.270
use TPU as equal to false
to a TPU estimator model,

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:17.684
you will get identical behavior
to standard estimators that run

00:27:17.684 --> 00:27:18.850
on all your other platforms.

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:20.520
So once you migrate
to TPU estimator,

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:23.730
it is very easy to run
on any platform you want,

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:27.945
CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs.

00:27:27.945 --> 00:27:29.570
Let's dig one level
deeper and give you

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:32.510
a bit of intuition
about this abstraction.

00:27:32.510 --> 00:27:36.170
The model function is
what runs on the TPU.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:40.550
This gets compiled by XLA
and goes really, really fast.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:43.220
The input function
runs on the x86 CPU

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:46.059
hosts that are attached.

00:27:46.059 --> 00:27:48.350
And now that you know sort
of a little bit of intuition

00:27:48.350 --> 00:27:49.970
about what's going
on, you can start

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:51.860
doing funny optimizations.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:54.200
Say, for example, you have
some input preprocessing that

00:27:54.200 --> 00:27:56.870
logically belongs in
the input function,

00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:58.820
but it's super
computationally expensive

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:01.060
and the CPUs just can't keep up.

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:04.100
Well, you can put that at the
top of your model function

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.680
and have that compiled
and run on the TPU.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:10.047
And as long as it
compiles from the TPU,

00:28:10.047 --> 00:28:11.630
can get all the
performance advantages

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:14.090
of running on the
accelerator device.

00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:16.010
By contrast, if
you have something

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.106
that can't compile
for the TPU, go ahead

00:28:18.106 --> 00:28:19.730
and hoist that into
your input function

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:21.760
with one key restriction.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:23.630
If you need to take
the gradient of it,

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:25.610
that has to happen in
the model function.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.160
It does have to go under
the TPU because that's

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:31.370
the way we do the cross shard
coordination is on the TPU

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:32.330
device itself.

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.190
We totally keep the
CPUs out of the path

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.740
because that's how we get the
amazing performance that we

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:37.310
see on TPUs.

00:28:40.172 --> 00:28:45.100
TPU estimator is designed
for production reliability,

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:46.890
scalability, and performance.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:49.140
It automatically
handles failures.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:52.860
So it does automatically saving
checkpoints and restoring

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:54.120
from the checkpoints.

00:28:54.120 --> 00:28:57.270
It has a very stable API that
doesn't change very much, even

00:28:57.270 --> 00:28:59.065
though it still is in contrib.

00:28:59.065 --> 00:29:00.440
There is very
little boilerplate.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:02.640
You just define your model
and your input function,

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:06.372
and construct your estimator,
you're off to the races.

00:29:06.372 --> 00:29:07.830
But we've heard
from a lot of folks

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:09.746
that although they really
liked TPU estimator,

00:29:09.746 --> 00:29:13.214
we've heard some other folks
that they don't quite like it.

00:29:13.214 --> 00:29:14.880
They want a little
bit more flexibility.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.980
They want a little
bit more control.

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:20.460
And so I'm really excited
to announce to you today

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:24.720
that Keras on cloud TPUs will
be available on TensorFlow 110.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.870
Our release candidates
for TF 110 just got--

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:30.074
first-release candidates
dropped just yesterday.

00:29:30.074 --> 00:29:31.740
But we're really
excited about what this

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:34.159
brings to our cloud TPU users.

00:29:34.159 --> 00:29:36.450
So let's talk about how this
Keras integration actually

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.020
works.

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:41.040
Here is a convolutional
network running

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:43.120
on standard Keras model.

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:45.420
It runs on CPUs or GPUs.

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:48.100
Let's convert it to run on TPUs.

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:49.110
OK, done.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:50.280
I didn't change anything.

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:53.200
You write your model
exactly as you did before.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:56.040
The only thing you
have to do is you

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.740
pass your existing
standard Keras model

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:00.764
to Keras support.tpu model.

00:30:00.764 --> 00:30:02.430
And you pass in a
couple of extra things

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:04.520
like your distribution
strategy and the cloud

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:06.150
TPU you want to run on.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:10.260
This will return to you
a TPU-ified Keras model.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.650
After that, that's it.

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:14.280
Use it just like a
standard Keras model.

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:17.092
Passing in num.py data,
passing a data set,

00:30:17.092 --> 00:30:18.300
do whatever you want with it.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:20.370
Call fit, call fit generator.

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:21.450
Call evaluate.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:23.550
Use it just like a
standard Keras model.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:25.620
We're really excited
about the flexibility

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:29.140
that this sort of an
API and Keras brings.

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:30.930
Keras is a really
great API and framework

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:31.880
from machine learning.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:33.720
A lot of people really like it.

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:37.490
Now it's even easier than ever
before to go to TPU hardware.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:42.090
So now that we have
multiple APIs, well,

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:43.550
how do you choose which one?

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.890
If you already have a model
that's using estimators,

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.170
definitely migrate
just to TPU estimator.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:53.550
This will give you
production, great performance.

00:30:53.550 --> 00:30:57.330
It's reliable, it's very
robust, it works very well.

00:30:57.330 --> 00:30:59.610
If you have a lot
of Keras models,

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:03.450
definitely try out our new
experimental Keras integration.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.760
This is a lot more
flexible, and people really

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.920
like it who have
tried it so far.

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:11.219
But if you haven't
started, I want

00:31:11.219 --> 00:31:12.510
to give you a bit of intuition.

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:14.270
If you're starting
something from scratch,

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:15.060
I want to give you
a bit of intuition

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:16.520
as to which API
you should choose.

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:21.010
One thing to note though.

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:23.910
The same team is developing
both TPU estimator and Keras.

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:26.650
And so although there are
current advantages to one

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:30.100
versus the other, expect
feature convergence over time.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:32.350
For now though, TPU
estimator scales

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.950
to whole pods with
zero code changes.

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:37.330
It's super fast, has
all the performance

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:39.420
optimizations in it.

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:42.220
Keras, by contrast, is a little
bit more flexible and easy

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:42.730
to use.

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:44.563
Error messages are often
a little bit easier

00:31:44.563 --> 00:31:45.450
to understand.

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:47.980
It supports non-static
shapes through a bunch

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:51.050
of dynamic recompilation
underneath the hood for you.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:53.290
So we're really excited
about both these two APIs.

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.540
If you want more
flexibility, go with Keras.

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:57.665
If you're in production,
you want peak performance,

00:31:57.665 --> 00:32:00.191
go with TPU estimator.

00:32:00.191 --> 00:32:01.690
Now we've gone
through the mechanics

00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:04.810
of how do you actually program
these cloud TPU devices.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:08.260
But as we've migrated a
huge number of customers

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.720
from other hardware
platforms to TPUs,

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.730
and we've done this both
internally at Google

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:15.580
and externally as
part of Google Cloud,

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:17.720
we've gleaned a number of
best practices and tips.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:21.250
So I'd like to walk you
through a few of them today.

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.390
Far and away the best advice we
can give you is, if possible,

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:27.430
start with a reference model.

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:31.750
These are well-tuned,
they're reasonably clean,

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:35.320
they are tested to
converge reliably.

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:37.252
So our RestNet-50
implementation,

00:32:37.252 --> 00:32:38.710
if you train it on
ImageNet, you're

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:43.005
going to get 76.2% top-1
accuracy every single time.

00:32:43.005 --> 00:32:45.130
These are high quality both
in the machine learning

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:47.810
and in the performance.

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:50.560
But if you can't, and
you have an existing code

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:53.230
base that you've invested in
for your machine learning model,

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:55.960
we have a workflow that
often works pretty well.

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:59.770
Step one is on your existing
hardware port to estimators.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:01.660
This often results in
a bunch of code getting

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.310
deleted that you
sort of manually

00:33:03.310 --> 00:33:06.544
were doing, like checkpoints
and things like that.

00:33:06.544 --> 00:33:07.960
And then at this
step, once you've

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:09.730
completed your
port to estimator,

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:11.005
be sure to verify convergence.

00:33:11.005 --> 00:33:14.206
This is often a mistake
that people skip.

00:33:14.206 --> 00:33:16.330
Once you're sure that your
model is working exactly

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:19.390
as you expect, then do
the mechanical change

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:21.610
from estimator to TPU estimator.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:23.440
Start with use TPU as
false, and make sure

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:26.060
that the mechanical
changes work just fine.

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:30.550
And after that, turn on TPUs
and you're off to the races.

00:33:30.550 --> 00:33:33.160
And finally, be sure to
optimize performance.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:35.246
You won't see the full
benefit of cloud TPUs

00:33:35.246 --> 00:33:37.870
if you ensure they're not being
bottle-necked by your CPU input

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:40.090
pipeline.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:42.520
Now this is a very common
workflow migrating existing

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.580
models to TPU hardware,
but we've actually

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:48.991
seen a lot of people just
start on cloud TPUs natively.

00:33:48.991 --> 00:33:51.490
There are a number of really
compelling advantages for that.

00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:55.104
In addition to being cheaper,
most importantly, they're fast.

00:33:55.104 --> 00:33:56.770
And so you can iterate
much more quickly

00:33:56.770 --> 00:34:00.995
than you can on any other
hardware platform that we see.

00:34:00.995 --> 00:34:03.370
So if you want to bring up a
brand new model from scratch

00:34:03.370 --> 00:34:06.070
and you're not starting from
one of our existing models,

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:09.510
be sure to iterate
very regularly.

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:12.699
As you-- it's much easier to
debug a few lines of code delta

00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:14.920
than it is a thousand-line
model function with a very

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:15.940
sophisticated network.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:19.302
If you're trying to figure
out your shape inference,

00:34:19.302 --> 00:34:20.260
you need static shapes.

00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:22.732
And shape inference isn't
working right, much easier

00:34:22.732 --> 00:34:25.190
to figure that out if you've
just changed a couple of lines

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:29.260
than if you have to hunt through
a thousand-line model function.

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:31.330
So iterate regularly.

00:34:31.330 --> 00:34:33.909
As a bonus tip, make sure to
optimize your input pipeline

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:35.590
performance early.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:37.960
This just is gas on
the iteration speed,

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:41.810
and just helps you go
a whole lot faster.

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.750
Once you've got a basic
thing working, usually

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:46.360
on a single cloud TPU
vise, take advantage

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:49.679
of what is the magic of
TPUs, which is you can scale.

00:34:49.679 --> 00:34:52.929
Scale these out to whole fleets
to run hyper parameter searches

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:57.680
in parallel, or scale up
to train your models super,

00:34:57.680 --> 00:34:58.450
super fast.

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:00.660
Get results, turnaround,
optimize your model,

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:05.480
and develop your system.

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:07.790
So there are some of the
high-level best practices.

00:35:07.790 --> 00:35:09.820
Let's dig down again
to the nitty gritty.

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:14.314
When you're developing models
on cloud TPUs, some of us

00:35:14.314 --> 00:35:15.230
aren't perfect coders.

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:17.284
We end up putting
bugs in our systems.

00:35:17.284 --> 00:35:19.450
And so I want to walk you
through some common errors

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:21.370
that people sometimes
stumble upon

00:35:21.370 --> 00:35:23.860
so that you have an
intuition for what's going on

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:26.870
and how to solve these
common issues that come up.

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:29.310
The first one is
unimplemented error.

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:31.730
This file scheme local
was not implemented.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:33.944
It tells you the file
that it's trying to do,

00:35:33.944 --> 00:35:35.360
and you'll grep
through your code,

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:37.360
and you're like nowhere
do I reference slash 10.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:39.380
What the heck's going on?

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:42.670
This is very common, because
estimators and therefore

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:45.092
TPU estimator, handles
software failures.

00:35:45.092 --> 00:35:46.300
It handles hardware failures.

00:35:46.300 --> 00:35:49.020
It handles other
problems in your system.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:51.370
And so as a result, it
requires a checkpoint directory

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:54.430
so it can save and
restore training progress.

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:56.980
And if you don't provide
at a checkpoint directory,

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:00.070
it will helpfully create
one for you on slash 10.

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:02.110
Now when you try and
run this on a cloud TPU,

00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:04.030
the cloud TPU is a
totally different device.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:06.870
It cannot read or write to
slash temp on your GCVM.

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:09.120
This will totally break all
the security abstractions,

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:11.030
like it just doesn't work.

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:13.660
And so what's happening
here is that it's

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:15.320
trying to do just that.

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:18.490
So the solution is to whenever
you're running a TPU estimator

00:36:18.490 --> 00:36:22.000
model on a cloud TPU, be sure
to specify the model directory.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:24.880
That's where your
checkpoints get saved to

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:26.664
and they get restored from.

00:36:26.664 --> 00:36:28.330
You need to set the
checkpoint directory

00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:30.040
to be someplace on
a shared file system

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:32.904
that both the cloud TPU
and your Python process

00:36:32.904 --> 00:36:33.820
can read and write to.

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:37.780
This is often GCS,
Google Cloud Storage.

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:40.120
Another common error
message is the following.

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:42.610
You get this NotFoundError:
No register 'Where'

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:45.130
OpKernel for XLA_TPU_JIT.

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:47.600
So what's going on here?

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:49.870
Well, remember that
TensorFlow graph

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:53.170
gets converted into an XLA
graph, which then gets compiled

00:36:53.170 --> 00:36:54.370
and runs on the TPU device.

00:36:54.370 --> 00:36:58.660
And what's happening is we don't
have a CF to XLA kernel for the

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:01.520
where op, OK?

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:04.150
And so what you
need to do is see

00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:07.300
if you can restructure your
program, your TensorFlow graph,

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:09.400
to not use these ops
that we don't have

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:11.770
a translation for into XLA.

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:14.127
Alternatively, you
could implement the T F

00:37:14.127 --> 00:37:16.460
to XLA kernel, because all
these are fully open sourced.

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:20.380
You can implement your
own, or file a bug

00:37:20.380 --> 00:37:22.210
and we'll see if we
can prioritize getting

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.780
those OpKernels implemented.

00:37:25.780 --> 00:37:28.150
OK, another common error
message that people might see

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:29.950
is the following.

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:31.900
NotFoundError: No
registered 'Cast' OpKernel

00:37:31.900 --> 00:37:33.160
for XLA_TPU_GIT.

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:35.300
Aha, we'll we've seen
this one before, right?

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:37.150
Well as it turns out,
it's a little subtle.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:41.050
The final parenthetical
is the key difference.

00:37:41.050 --> 00:37:42.470
The OpKernel was found.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:45.097
So there is a TF
to XLA OpKernel,

00:37:45.097 --> 00:37:46.430
but the attributes didn't match.

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:48.460
So let's dig into what
are the attributes.

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:51.780
We've got our destination
type of DT int16.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:54.460
Our source type, DT
float, and a device.

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:56.150
Now the device is fine.

00:37:56.150 --> 00:38:00.050
The issue is actually DT int16.

00:38:00.050 --> 00:38:01.900
TPUs don't support int16.

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:07.090
They only support float32 or
DT float, int32, et cetera.

00:38:07.090 --> 00:38:09.280
So the solution
here is to, instead

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:11.960
of trying to cast to
int16, leave it as float,

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:13.850
or cast to int32.

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:16.750
If you want to see what
ops a priori, before you

00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:19.510
get these error messages, if you
want to see what ops are likely

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:22.400
compatible with TPUs or not,
we have this really cool TPU

00:38:22.400 --> 00:38:23.740
compatibility checker.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:27.430
And the tools page
documents really clearly

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:29.170
how you can use it.

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:30.340
Now one thing to note.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.920
We're constantly adding
new kernel implementations

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:35.590
and supporting new
data types, et cetera.

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:38.770
And so the source
of truth is often

00:38:38.770 --> 00:38:42.800
the compiler and the TensorFlow
version you're actually using.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:45.460
So be aware that there is
some delta between what

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:49.680
the tool says and the reality.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:52.310
The final error message that
people often stumble over

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:54.730
is the following.

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:57.960
NotFoundError: Op type
not registered 'PyFunc.'

00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:01.110
Now remember, cloud TPUs are
this distributed computation

00:39:01.110 --> 00:39:01.620
device.

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:06.300
Your Python runs on your
GCVM, but the cloud TPU

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:09.150
has no Python available
to it on the CPU.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:12.690
It's just a C++ binary,
as it turns out.

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:16.350
And so you can't use
PyFuncs on your cloud TPU.

00:39:16.350 --> 00:39:18.300
However, with a little
bit of cleverness,

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:20.310
you can use PyFuncs in
your input function,

00:39:20.310 --> 00:39:23.460
like we are here, our
input pipeline task zero.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:25.440
You can do that
with a technique I'm

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:28.230
going to show you in the
next section, which brings me

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.480
to feeding a TPU program.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:35.690
So I want to take a step back
before we delve into this

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:39.880
and actually remark upon a trend
that is happening across TPUs.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:43.180
In fact, if you look
at Nvidia's Volta GPUs,

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.730
they're phenomenal accelerators.

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:45.810
They're really fast.

00:39:45.810 --> 00:39:48.600
And Nvidia's done a great
job working on them.

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:51.360
And if you try to run a
fast model on Nvidia Volta

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:55.770
GPUs, like RestNet-50,
on a GG x-1,

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:58.650
you'll find that the
local SSD can't keep up.

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:00.990
We've seen this in
TensorFlow as we benchmark

00:40:00.990 --> 00:40:02.940
on a wide variety of
hardware platforms

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:05.610
that unless you have a
high-performance storage

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:08.220
system, your
accelerators are actually

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:11.220
going to sit idle waiting
for data to be fed in.

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:13.620
And in this case, just the
raw throughput of the SSD

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:14.500
can't keep up.

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:17.200
If you have the data in memory
on your RAMDisk you're fine.

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:18.575
But if it's on
the SSD and you've

00:40:18.575 --> 00:40:23.380
got a cold RAMDisk or a cold
buffer cache, you're not.

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:26.680
And if you try and
optimize around this,

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:28.750
it can often be quite pricey.

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:31.540
So one company found
that the storage costs

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:34.930
alone on a non-GCP
platform was more

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:39.170
expensive than the entire cloud
TPU solution including storage,

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:40.910
including the cloud
TPUs themselves,

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:43.670
in this case it was
preemptible including the GCVMs

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:44.570
to drive it.

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:47.890
So the entire GC
solution was cheaper

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:49.750
than just storage on
this other platform.

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:51.580
The GPUs could be
completely free.

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.244
The compute could
be completely free.

00:40:53.244 --> 00:40:55.660
The VMs, the compute, everything
could be completely free.

00:40:55.660 --> 00:40:58.770
Just the storage alone
was more expensive.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:00.460
And so to tell you
about how you can

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:06.044
feed fast accelerators, TPUs,
but also GPUs on Google Cloud

00:41:06.044 --> 00:41:07.210
platform, this is available.

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:09.666
All these are available
in standard TensorFlow.

00:41:09.666 --> 00:41:13.110
I want to walk you through
three ways that you can do this.

00:41:13.110 --> 00:41:15.940
So the first and most common
way is Google Cloud Storage.

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:18.010
This is a distributed
file system

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:20.500
that is optimized for
both extreme scale

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:21.835
and shockingly low cost.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:27.160
Whoops.

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:29.650
The next one that people often
do is use Compute Engine.

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:33.700
This allows you to stream
data from either local SSDs

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:36.046
or in-memory data structures.

00:41:36.046 --> 00:41:37.420
This is where you
can use PyFuncs

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:38.710
to preprocess your data.

00:41:38.710 --> 00:41:43.270
You can stream the data
from GCVMs into a cloud TPU.

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:46.060
And finally, I'm really
excited to announce a brand-new

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:49.340
integration coming in TensorFlow
1.10 over the next few weeks.

00:41:49.340 --> 00:41:51.252
That is Cloud Bigtable.

00:41:51.252 --> 00:41:54.200
Cloud Bigtable, for those
of you who are not familiar,

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:57.850
is a wide-row, structured
storage system.

00:41:57.850 --> 00:42:01.480
Scales out horizontally,
and has amazing performance

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:04.580
both for low latency as
well as high throughput,

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:08.954
which is relatively unique
for a system of this caliber.

00:42:08.954 --> 00:42:10.870
So to walk you through
what these all are like

00:42:10.870 --> 00:42:13.990
and how to use them,
let's dig right in.

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:16.570
Cloud storage is definitely
the default choice.

00:42:16.570 --> 00:42:18.070
It's a familiar abstraction.

00:42:18.070 --> 00:42:20.470
Files on it should
be the file system.

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:22.540
People are-- easy, it's
very easy to work with.

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:24.170
Has very nice UIs
and integrations

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.692
with a wide variety
of different tools.

00:42:26.692 --> 00:42:28.150
I have some tips
and tricks for you

00:42:28.150 --> 00:42:30.580
to make sure you can get
the best performance out

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:32.770
of Google Cloud Storage.

00:42:32.770 --> 00:42:36.040
The first one is make sure
you select regional storage,

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:39.294
so not multi-regional, not
near-line, not cold-line,

00:42:39.294 --> 00:42:40.960
or else you're going
to get a nasty bill

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:44.137
at the end of the month,
because those end up

00:42:44.137 --> 00:42:46.720
being far more expensive for use
with high-performance compute

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:47.720
devices like cloud TPUs.

00:42:47.720 --> 00:42:50.980
Additionally, make
sure that your data

00:42:50.980 --> 00:42:53.500
is in the exact same region
as your accelerators, US

00:42:53.500 --> 00:43:00.690
central one or Europe
west four, or in Taiwan.

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:04.160
Additionally, GCS is optimized
for high throughput but not

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:05.510
low latency.

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:07.360
And so in order to
work around, in order

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:08.901
to achieve high
performance, you need

00:43:08.901 --> 00:43:13.240
to batch your data into
large often TF record files.

00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:15.530
We've found that 100
megabytes to 200 megabytes

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:17.540
works very well to
allow-- it's a sort

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:20.900
of a sweet spot between
parallelism and throughput

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:23.250
of the files.

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:26.110
To handle all this complicated
data loading and parallelism

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:29.200
and software pipelining,
TF data makes it very easy.

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:32.300
So let's walk
through an example.

00:43:32.300 --> 00:43:36.360
Here we are loading a
set of TF record files

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:39.360
in this case from a GCS path.

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:41.676
We're reading 32
files in parallel.

00:43:41.676 --> 00:43:43.050
And after that,
we've constructed

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:45.060
a data set that we can
then preprocess or do

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:46.770
whatever we want.

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:48.900
This works, of course,
for cloud TPUs,

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:52.560
but GPUs on GC, et cetera.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:53.130
That's it.

00:43:53.130 --> 00:43:54.088
Just two lines of code.

00:43:54.088 --> 00:43:56.370
It's pretty easy.

00:43:56.370 --> 00:43:59.070
Next one I want to talk
about is Compute Engine VMs.

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:00.750
We have a few
different ways that you

00:44:00.750 --> 00:44:03.790
can feed data into cloud
TPUs at high performance.

00:44:03.790 --> 00:44:07.050
The first one is the
streaming files data set.

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.510
This is a software
pipeline data in feed

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:12.510
that you can read from
local disk, often local SSD,

00:44:12.510 --> 00:44:16.700
because that's faster
than persistent disk SSD.

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:18.367
But you can feed from both.

00:44:18.367 --> 00:44:19.950
With streaming files,
you can actually

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:23.190
use arbitrary
Python to preprocess

00:44:23.190 --> 00:44:25.240
the data using
PyFuncs or otherwise

00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:28.210
to stream it into a cloud TPU.

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:30.380
Now often we've found if
you do try and use PyFuncs,

00:44:30.380 --> 00:44:31.980
that will be a
performance bottleneck.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:33.900
But for certain
models, this absolutely

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:34.850
makes a lot of sense.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:36.450
It's also great as
an experimentation

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:39.030
or getting started tool.

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:42.780
Additionally, you have a second
method, which is this RPC op.

00:44:42.780 --> 00:44:46.140
You can actually load data
from any arbitrary GRPC server

00:44:46.140 --> 00:44:49.710
as long as you implement
a small GRPC interface.

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:52.350
This works really well when data
is being generated on the fly,

00:44:52.350 --> 00:44:54.540
especially if data is stored
in memory, for example,

00:44:54.540 --> 00:44:57.907
in reinforcement learning
type style workloads.

00:44:57.907 --> 00:44:59.990
So to give you an illustration
of what's going on,

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:01.790
you have a Compute
Engine VM, and you're

00:45:01.790 --> 00:45:04.880
passing the streaming
files data set from the VM,

00:45:04.880 --> 00:45:08.230
streaming the data from
the VM into the cloud TPU.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:11.640
Now TensorFlow is designed as
a distributed execution engine.

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:14.840
So you can actually stream
data from multiple GCVMs

00:45:14.840 --> 00:45:16.202
into one cloud TPU.

00:45:16.202 --> 00:45:19.980
Or of course, you can use the
RPC op for one or more GCVMs.

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:22.830
To take a look at some code to
see how you'd actually do this,

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:25.280
here's an example of the
streaming files data set.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:27.800
We're reading from a
local path on disk.

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:29.560
In this case, we're
doing TF records.

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:32.630
But if you wanted to do more
complicated preprocessing,

00:45:32.630 --> 00:45:35.120
you can pass in a
few extra arguments

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:36.800
and you're off to the races.

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:37.550
One thing to note.

00:45:37.550 --> 00:45:39.800
You do have to configure
your TPU cluster resolver

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:41.390
a little bit
differently in order

00:45:41.390 --> 00:45:45.960
to ensure that the cloud TPU
knows where to find your GCVM

00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:49.020
to pull the data from.

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:51.530
And finally, I'd like to talk
a few minutes about Cloud

00:45:51.530 --> 00:45:54.330
Bigtable.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:56.960
In some benchmarks,
Cloud Bigtable

00:45:56.960 --> 00:45:59.630
has been shown to be over ten
times faster than Cloud Google

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:00.710
Storage.

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:03.840
So it's this amazing
performance system.

00:46:03.840 --> 00:46:07.190
And because it's a database,
high-scale database,

00:46:07.190 --> 00:46:08.960
you can have a
flexible schema and you

00:46:08.960 --> 00:46:11.520
can query your data in a
variety of different patterns.

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:14.390
So it's really valuable for
experimentation or other sorts

00:46:14.390 --> 00:46:15.860
of workloads.

00:46:15.860 --> 00:46:18.440
Additionally, if you're
generating your data

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:20.480
on the fly, from
say multiple worker

00:46:20.480 --> 00:46:23.270
VMs you're doing like self-play
in a reinforcement learning

00:46:23.270 --> 00:46:25.947
algorithm, you can stream
the data into Bigtable

00:46:25.947 --> 00:46:28.280
and you don't have to create
your own batch record files

00:46:28.280 --> 00:46:29.300
or anything like that.

00:46:29.300 --> 00:46:32.420
Bigtable handles all of
that for you automatically.

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:34.620
I have a sample script
available on GitHub

00:46:34.620 --> 00:46:36.710
for how to load TF
records into Bigtable,

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:38.720
but it's designed to
be easily flexible

00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:40.910
and rewritten to stream
data of whatever format

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:42.950
you want into Bigtable.

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:46.730
Getting data out of Bigtable is
also really easy with TF data.

00:46:46.730 --> 00:46:50.150
You create a Bigtable
client, you open the table,

00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:54.170
and then you can scan rows
either by range or by prefix,

00:46:54.170 --> 00:46:56.240
specifying whatever
column families

00:46:56.240 --> 00:46:58.080
and columns you want out.

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:00.780
This gives you a data set that
you can use just like normal.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.890
So with TF data, it's very
flexible and very easy

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:04.640
to switch between all
these different data

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:08.780
sources while maintaining
your preprocessing operations,

00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:11.030
et cetera, lower down
in your input function.

00:47:13.790 --> 00:47:16.850
Now that we have choices,
well how do you choose?

00:47:16.850 --> 00:47:18.500
This walks through
sort of the pricing

00:47:18.500 --> 00:47:20.820
of these different
storage solutions.

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:23.880
So Google Cloud Storage is
$0.02 per gigabyte per month

00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:25.760
and is the cheapest.

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:30.300
The other storage systems have
different prices as listed.

00:47:30.300 --> 00:47:32.870
Google Cloud Storage, you
don't pay unless you're

00:47:32.870 --> 00:47:34.250
pulling data out of it.

00:47:34.250 --> 00:47:38.150
So the hourly overheads are
zero, which is really nice.

00:47:38.150 --> 00:47:41.570
On the second half of
the slide of this table,

00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:43.740
I walk through sort of
the pricing example.

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:47.300
If you have a one
terabyte training data set

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:49.670
and you train on
it 10 hours, here's

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:52.739
your costs for just the storage
and for everything all-in,

00:47:52.739 --> 00:47:54.280
whether you're using
a standard cloud

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:55.940
TPU or a preemptible cloud TPU.

00:47:58.930 --> 00:48:02.400
So that's an overview of
our three main areas, what's

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:05.010
going on underneath the
hood of a cloud CPU,

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:07.920
how to program a cloud
TPU using our new APIs,

00:48:07.920 --> 00:48:09.760
and finally, how to feed data.

00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:13.410
But I want to take this all back
to really the whole purpose,

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:16.165
which is you.

00:48:16.165 --> 00:48:19.470
We've talked about how to use
cloud TPUs on Compute Engine,

00:48:19.470 --> 00:48:23.160
but you can also use it on Cloud
ML engine or Kubernetes Engine.

00:48:23.160 --> 00:48:24.780
Cloud ML Engine
makes it very, very

00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:27.360
easy to switch between
CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs.

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:33.282
Just change one flag for your
model and resubmit your job.

00:48:33.282 --> 00:48:34.740
Kubernetes has this
new integration

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:36.865
where you can request a
cloud TPU to be provisioned

00:48:36.865 --> 00:48:39.840
and de-provisioned for
you as part of your pods

00:48:39.840 --> 00:48:42.710
when your pods get
launched and deleted

00:48:42.710 --> 00:48:44.740
on the Kubernetes cluster.

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:46.919
So these higher-level
APIs are really nice.

00:48:46.919 --> 00:48:48.460
They handle a lot
of the convenience.

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:50.520
You don't have to worry about
network ranges, et cetera.

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:52.140
So really excited
to have all three

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:54.270
of these different
engines available to you

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:58.590
for your workloads for
whatever your style of work is.

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:02.040
But I want to take a step
back and realize, well,

00:49:02.040 --> 00:49:05.202
what are TPUs really.

00:49:05.202 --> 00:49:07.410
And I want to sort of take
a different interpretation

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:09.000
of things.

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:12.450
This is a plot that shows
the performance of a model

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:16.140
as it scales across a
single cloud TPU device

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:20.910
up to a whole second-generation
cloud TPU pod of 64 devices.

00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:23.130
On the dotted line
is perfect scaling,

00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:25.782
and the blue line is the
observed performance.

00:49:25.782 --> 00:49:27.240
Now this is a cool
chart that shows

00:49:27.240 --> 00:49:31.380
like yes, wahoo, our custom
mesh network is super fast.

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:34.440
But what this means for you
is that you write your model

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:37.540
on a single cloud TPU device.

00:49:37.540 --> 00:49:40.140
And with zero code
changes, you scale it up

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:42.060
to a whole cloud TPU pod.

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:44.760
And you get your answers
linearly times faster

00:49:44.760 --> 00:49:47.070
in many cases.

00:49:47.070 --> 00:49:50.040
This means that TPUs
are like a time machine.

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:52.950
Instead of having to
wait days or weeks,

00:49:52.950 --> 00:49:55.980
you can iterate in
hours or even faster.

00:49:55.980 --> 00:50:00.210
And this just supercharges
both ML research as well as

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:03.610
ML product development.

00:50:03.610 --> 00:50:05.920
We're bringing all
this technology to you

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:08.620
as we optimize from our
storage systems, our compute,

00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:10.540
our network, and
our TPU devices.

00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:13.330
We're bringing all this
great technology to you.

00:50:13.330 --> 00:50:14.920
The sky's the limit.

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:17.650
Develop your
applications ethically.

00:50:17.650 --> 00:50:19.852
Conduct your
research responsibly.

00:50:19.852 --> 00:50:22.060
But I'm super excited to
see what you all will build.

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.726
Thank you very much
for your time today.

00:50:23.726 --> 00:50:26.134
